WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:07.026
FedFound: a federated foundation model for gastrointestinal
endoscopy imaging.

00:00:07.476 --> 00:00:17.652
From West Virginia University with collaborators at Rowan,
Aberdeen and BioImaginix, recently accepted to npj Digital

00:00:19.002 --> 00:00:27.018
Endoscopy is demanding work, and deep learning can help — but
the models that work best are supervised.

00:00:27.468 --> 00:00:35.220
Supervision here means an expert endoscopist labelling video
frame by frame, which is slow and expensive.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:44.646
Worse, a model trained to detect polyps does not transfer to
grading inflammation. Each task needs its own labelled set.

00:00:45.096 --> 00:00:51.072
And no single hospital has enough labelled data to train a
strong model on its own.

00:00:51.522 --> 00:01:00.210
A foundation model changes the shape of that problem: learn
once from unlabelled images, then adapt with very few labels.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:10.536
Foundation models need large and diverse data in one place,
and in medicine that is exactly what you cannot have.

00:01:10.986 --> 00:01:23.394
Endoscopic images sit in silos at individual hospitals,
governed by privacy regulation, differing data governance

00:01:23.844 --> 00:01:33.684
Collectively these institutions generate an enormous amount of
endoscopy. Individually, none has enough to train a foundation

00:01:34.134 --> 00:01:43.278
Federated learning is the standard answer to that shape of
problem: keep the data where it is, and exchange only model

00:01:43.728 --> 00:01:57.672
But federated learning has mostly been applied to task-
specific supervised training. Doing task-agnostic, self-

00:01:59.022 --> 00:02:08.406
Start with one hospital. It holds an endoscopy archive that
cannot leave the building, and it trains a local copy of the

00:02:08.856 --> 00:02:13.416
Add a second, with a different scope and a different patient
population.

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:20.730
And a third, and in principle any number — the paper uses five
clients plus a server.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:30.060
Each computes the difference between its trained weights and
the model it started from, delta theta, and sends only that to

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:37.710
The server aggregates those updates into a single global
model. That is the federated foundation model.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.416
It is then handed on for fine-tuning — and at no point did an
image move.

00:02:44.766 --> 00:02:53.214
What each client actually trains on is a masked autoencoding
task. Take an endoscopic frame and hide part of it.

00:02:53.664 --> 00:02:58.104
The visible remainder goes through a Vision Transformer
encoder.

00:02:58.554 --> 00:03:03.906
That compresses it to a latent representation — far smaller
than the image.

00:03:04.356 --> 00:03:07.332
A decoder expands the latent back out.

00:03:07.782 --> 00:03:15.318
And the training signal is how well the reconstruction matches
the patch that was hidden, under an L1 loss.

00:03:15.768 --> 00:03:29.136
To fill in a hidden region of mucosa the model has to learn
texture, vasculature and the geometry of the lumen. That is

00:03:30.486 --> 00:03:39.462
Once pretraining is done, the backbone is frozen work for
three downstream tasks, each with only a small labelled set.

00:03:39.912 --> 00:03:51.648
Classification on Gastrovision: twenty-seven classes covering
anatomical landmarks, pathological findings, polyp removal

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:58.818
Object detection on EDD2020: bounding boxes over five
pathological classes.

00:03:59.268 --> 00:04:04.740
And instance segmentation on the same data, producing lesion
masks.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:13.350
Three clinically distinct tasks from one shared representation
— that is the claim the rest of the paper tests.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.652
Mechanically a round has four steps, and it is worth being
precise about what moves.

00:04:21.102 --> 00:04:27.150
The server broadcasts the current global model to every
participating hospital.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:35.520
Each hospital runs local masked-autoencoding steps on its own
images — the data never leaves the building.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:41.562
Each returns only the difference between its trained weights
and the model it started from.

00:04:42.012 --> 00:04:49.812
The server averages those updates into a new global model, and
the cycle repeats a thousand times.

00:04:51.162 --> 00:04:55.914
Here is what the server is actually doing, on the loss
surface.

00:04:56.364 --> 00:05:01.476
Every client starts from the same broadcast model — one point
on the landscape.

00:05:01.926 --> 00:05:14.622
But each descends toward whichever minimum its own data
favours. A hospital with capsule endoscopy walks somewhere

00:05:15.072 --> 00:05:22.104
FedAvg takes the mean of where they ended up, which can land
between the basins rather than in any of them.

00:05:22.554 --> 00:05:31.410
That is why the paper tests four server rules — momentum, Adam
and Adagrad all change how this averaged step is applied.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:40.908
And it is why the heterogeneous split is the harder test: the
more the clients disagree, the worse a plain average behaves.

00:05:42.258 --> 00:05:47.754
Before the numbers, the evaluation design, because it
determines what the numbers mean.

00:05:48.204 --> 00:05:56.436
The lower bound is a model pretrained only on the server's own
share of data — what one institution could achieve alone.

00:05:56.886 --> 00:06:07.950
The upper bound is pretrained on everything pooled centrally —
what you could achieve if privacy were not a constraint. It is

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:14.064
There is also a model with no pretraining at all, to show that
pretraining matters.

00:06:14.514 --> 00:06:24.210
The question the whole paper answers is how much of the gap
between one hospital's model and the centralised ceiling

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:35.808
Pretraining uses two datasets across five clients and a
server: HyperKvasir, standard endoscopy, and Kvasir-Capsule.

00:06:36.258 --> 00:06:47.946
On the left, the homogeneous split. Both datasets are pooled
and divided evenly, so every client holds about 23,000 images

00:06:48.396 --> 00:06:59.412
On the right, the heterogeneous split. Kvasir-Capsule goes
entirely to two clients, HyperKvasir to the other three, and

00:06:59.862 --> 00:07:11.574
The two datasets differ in imaging device, resolution, focus
region within the GI tract and temporal structure, so those

00:07:12.024 --> 00:07:21.120
That is the realistic case. A hospital does not receive a
random sample of the world's endoscopy — it has the scopes it

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:27.798
On the homogeneous split, classification accuracy runs as
follows.

00:07:28.248 --> 00:07:40.776
No pretraining, 68.72 percent. The lower bound, 72.62 — so
pretraining on even one site's data is worth almost four

00:07:41.226 --> 00:07:53.010
FedFoundAvg reaches 78.40 percent, beating the lower bound by
5.78 points and landing 3.3 short of the centralised ceiling.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:08:06.516
The other three aggregation rules all help, but less: Adagrad
75.40, AvgM 74.84, Adam 73.20.

00:08:06.966 --> 00:08:15.558
Plain FedAvg winning is a genuinely useful finding — the
simplest aggregation rule is also the best one here.

00:08:16.908 --> 00:08:22.908
Under heterogeneity the picture sharpens, and in the direction
that matters.

00:08:23.358 --> 00:08:32.142
The lower bound drops to 71.06 percent, because a single
site's data is now less representative.

00:08:32.592 --> 00:08:41.952
FedFoundAvg reaches 77.48 — a gain of 6.42 points, larger than
in the homogeneous case.

00:08:42.402 --> 00:08:50.322
So federation helps more precisely when clients differ, which
is the situation any real deployment would face.

00:08:50.772 --> 00:09:02.028
One honest wrinkle: FedAdam actually falls slightly below the
lower bound here at 70.86, so adaptive server optimisers are

00:09:03.378 --> 00:09:09.546
The same pattern holds beyond classification, which is what
the foundation-model claim requires.

00:09:09.996 --> 00:09:22.980
For object detection under heterogeneity, the lower bound
reaches mAP of 11.95; FedFoundAvg reaches 19.07 — a gain of

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:34.398
For instance segmentation, the lower bound collapses to a Dice
score of 0.565 while FedFoundAvg achieves 0.662.

00:09:34.848 --> 00:09:45.960
That segmentation number is worth pausing on: 0.662 is
essentially identical to the centralised upper bound of 0.663.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:53.706
On that task, federated pretraining recovers the full benefit
of pooling the data — without pooling it.

00:09:55.056 --> 00:10:02.088
Laid out together: classification, detection, segmentation,
under the heterogeneous split.

00:10:02.538 --> 00:10:08.298
Every FedFound number sits between the lower and upper bounds,
closer to the upper.

00:10:08.748 --> 00:10:17.988
All figures are averaged over five independent fine-tuning
runs with different seeds, and the paper reports standard

00:10:18.438 --> 00:10:29.142
They also run Wilcoxon signed-rank tests against the lower
bound, and are explicit that the reported p-values are

00:10:30.492 --> 00:10:39.924
A real federation has more than five participants, so they re-
split the same fixed dataset across ten and then twenty nodes.

00:10:40.374 --> 00:10:48.726
The total volume is held constant. What changes is
fragmentation — each client's slice gets smaller and more

00:10:49.176 --> 00:10:58.728
These are the resulting partitions: at twenty nodes several
clients hold only a few thousand images, and the mix varies

00:10:59.178 --> 00:11:03.498
That is a much harder federated problem than the five-client
setting.

00:11:04.848 --> 00:11:09.144
With the data that fragmented, the numbers separate sharply.

00:11:09.594 --> 00:11:18.882
The single-site lower bound manages 38.18 percent accuracy at
ten nodes and 40.42 at twenty.

00:11:19.332 --> 00:11:28.500
FedFoundAvg holds at 72.34 and 73.26 percent — roughly double.

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:43.134
Detection and segmentation move the same way: mAP goes from
8.27 to 15.10 at ten nodes, and Dice from 0.272 to 0.661.

00:11:43.584 --> 00:11:48.432
That gap is the clearest argument in the paper for federating
at all.

00:11:49.782 --> 00:11:57.414
The strongest test is external validation on datasets that
played no part in pretraining or fine-tuning.

00:11:57.864 --> 00:12:12.144
PolypGen spans six centres across Egypt, France, Italy, Norway
and the UK. CP-CHILD-B is paediatric colonoscopy, average

00:12:12.594 --> 00:12:28.602
On PolypGen, federated pretraining nearly doubles accuracy
over the lower bound — 43.09 against 23.48 — and F1 rises from

00:12:29.052 --> 00:12:34.260
On CP-CHILD-B the accuracy gains are small, though F1
improves.

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:49.710
Read this carefully, though. The direction is right and
consistent, but 43 percent accuracy on PolypGen is a long way

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:55.452
Qualitatively, the difference shows up as coherence.

00:12:55.902 --> 00:13:08.838
The lower bound tends to produce fragmented predictions,
imprecise localisation and incomplete masks, especially in

00:13:09.288 --> 00:13:15.000
FedFoundAvg produces more coherent detections and smoother
masks.

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:27.498
The paper is candid that it still struggles when surgical
instruments are in frame, or under uneven illumination — and

00:13:28.848 --> 00:13:35.160
Convergence behaviour separates the aggregation rules more
clearly than the final numbers do.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:43.242
The centralised upper bound rises fast and plateaus early, as
you would expect from pooled data.

00:13:43.692 --> 00:13:48.732
The lower bound climbs slowly throughout, still learning at
the end.

00:13:49.182 --> 00:13:59.526
FedFoundAvg rises steeply and stabilises early at high
accuracy — fast and efficient, if still short of centralised.

00:13:59.976 --> 00:14:09.744
The other federated methods track the lower bound and only
overtake it late, which suggests slower aggregation of

00:14:11.094 --> 00:14:14.790
The costs are reported openly, and they are substantial.

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:24.312
Because the full foundation model is transmitted each round,
each client sends and receives about 896 megabytes per round.

00:14:24.762 --> 00:14:32.442
Over a thousand rounds with six clients, that is roughly 5,250
gigabytes of traffic.

00:14:32.892 --> 00:14:43.212
Training took about two days and fifteen hours with clients
run sequentially, and peaked at 45 gigabytes of GPU memory per

00:14:43.662 --> 00:14:55.686
The authors point to low-rank adapters, quantisation and
periodic transmission as the routes to reducing this, and

00:14:57.036 --> 00:15:01.908
Four things worth keeping in view, all of which the paper
itself discusses.

00:15:02.358 --> 00:15:16.734
The federation is simulated: both pretraining datasets come
from one Norwegian hospital and are partitioned to create

00:15:17.184 --> 00:15:27.720
External accuracy is modest in absolute terms — forty-three
percent on PolypGen, where even centralised pretraining

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:34.938
The federated setting is idealised, with synchronous rounds
and no client dropouts modelled.

00:15:35.388 --> 00:15:45.180
And privacy is argued structurally rather than tested;
evaluating attacks such as gradient inversion was outside the

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:56.706
To summarise: federated learning works for foundation-model
pretraining, not only for the task-specific supervised

00:15:57.156 --> 00:16:08.196
Masked autoencoding across silos recovers most of what
centralised pretraining would give you, and on instance

00:16:08.646 --> 00:16:16.422
The advantage grows as data becomes more fragmented, which is
the direction real deployments run in.

00:16:16.872 --> 00:16:24.768
And the simplest server rule, plain averaging, beat momentum
and both adaptive variants almost everywhere.

